THE HAZARD, and why this is not an ordinary refactor:
XML.Encode.variant (xml.ML:324) and XML.Decode.variant (xml.scala:483)
key on the POSITIONAL INDEX of the encoder/decoder in the list, and
every nullary scalar encodes to the same bytes (empty attributes, empty
body). a scala list in the wrong order therefore decodes Nat as Int
SILENTLY — no exception, just a wrong json type. no scala-unit test can
catch it (Fake rows never cross the encoder); only a bridge case
comparing a live ML declaration against the decoded row can. see A5.

and there is NO COMPATIBILITY WINDOW: a stale mcp.jar against a rebuilt
heap fails inside XML.Decode with an XML_Body exception, not with a
clean error, and it surfaces as a dead tools/list rather than a
diagnostic. the two sides change in ONE commit (commits.md rule 3
forces code+tests together anyway), and the step's verification order
is: edit both sides -> scala_build -> isabelle build -> mcp_test -b.

constraints do NOT cross the wire: Exactly_One drives the runtime check
(ML) and a sentence appended to the description at tool-construction
time (ML), so the description that crosses already carries it. one
rule, one implementation, nothing for a second surface to drift from.

