runtime semantics
------------------

- check_value becomes an exhaustive match on ptyp. Enum items:
  membership, or a typed error naming the param AND listing the items.
  List_Of t: check each element against t.
- LIST ARGUMENTS ARRIVE AS REPEATED KEYS — the existing convention
  (mcp_server.scala:1012 json_args: a json array becomes repeated
  (key, element) pairs in array order; mcp_session.scala:148 documents
  it; pass_args at :714 consumes it for scope_add and repl_init). so
  validate stops being able to use AList.lookup for a List_Of param (it
  takes the first occurrence only) and collects every occurrence,
  returning them as repeated pairs so the tool's run function sees the
  whole list. absent = zero occurrences. assemble's "quoted"
  (MCP_Tools.thy:567) has the SAME first-occurrence bug and needs the
  same fix — one reasoning, two call sites, easy to miss twice.
- validate grows the constraint list as an argument
  (validate ctxt params constraints args) rather than getting a sibling
  check_constraints the callers could forget. Exactly_One ns counts
  members present in the RAW args, before defaults are filled (a
  default would make one member permanently present); zero, or more
  than one, is an error naming the members.
- assemble: Enum substitutes VERBATIM (see spec refinements), List_Of t
  quotes each element per t and joins with a single space, an absent
  optional substitutes the empty string.
- MCP_Combinators.param survives the deletion of param_types with a NEW
  job — it is the one registration-time gate on a param, now checking
  what the type system cannot: enum items nonempty, distinct and
  token-safe (no whitespace, quote or cartouche character — this is
  what makes the verbatim splice defensible rather than merely
  convenient); a "= default" on an enum is one of the items; a List_Of
  param carries NO default (rejected at registration rather than
  inventing a list-literal default syntax the spec never specced —
  recorded as deferred).
- tool construction appends the constraint sentence to the description:
  "Exactly one of offset, pattern, index is required." — the spec's
  exact wording, pinned by assertion.

